+# UI3 — native full screen, 2026-10-06
+
+## Request and diagnosis
+
+Ibrahim reported that expanding Baton enlarged a normal window rather than entering native full screen, with a sticky menu during expansion. CUA reproduced zoom on the ordinary SwiftUI window, ID links: the green control remained a zoom button and the window retained rounded corners/native controls after expansion. The earlier UI2 live observation had covered the manually created review window, not this ordinary scene; it was insufficient evidence for both paths.
+
+The ordinary scene’s WindowGlassChrome applied only glass properties; only the manual review window applied fullScreenPrimary. It also reapplied styleMask/titlebar/appearance properties on every SwiftUI update. UI3 routes both paths through the same native configuration, removes conflicting fullScreenAuxiliary/fullScreenNone flags while preserving unrelated behavior, and makes property changes conditional so normal view updates leave styles/frame untouched. No delegate or app-wide presentation setting is replaced.
+
+## Files
+
+- app/Sources/BatonUI/Components/WindowPresentation.swift
+- app/Tests/BatonUITests/WindowInteractionTests.swift
+- docs/plan/STATUS.md
+- docs/plan/track-d-app.md
+- docs/plan/reports/UI3-2026-10-06.md
+
+## Verification and challenge
+
+Two native regression tests cover the ordinary scene probe’s full-screen capability and repeated chrome configuration without style/frame mutation. Both fail on the previous implementation (four assertions total), and pass with the correction; all 13 window interaction tests pass. Full checks pass: 377 engine, 73 app (56 UI and 17 Kit in the new per-product runner), Swift build. No sentences were added outside the catalogue; scans only match existing labels or NSColor identifiers. Source diff whitespace check passes.
+
+## Toolchain limitation and local check selection
+
+This session has Apple Swift 6.4 and its new swiftbuild default. Unmodified baseline `swift build`/`swift test` fail to resolve macro plugins from the macOS 27 SDK; this is not introduced by UI3. The deprecated native engine also cannot find Testing with its default configuration. Checks succeed with the already installed macOS 26.5 SDK and an explicit installed TestingMacros library passed only on the command line. No installation, system selection change or package dependency was made. The plain default commands remain an environment limitation; claims of a green app refer to the exact working commands pasted below.
+
+## Native fixture check
+
+Rebuilt executable and resource bundle copied to the already authorized /private/tmp/BatonFixture.app. Quit only the owned old fixture using Command-Q (the AX call timed out after exit; application inventory no longer listed it). Both elevated `open -n /private/tmp/BatonFixture.app --args --review-window` attempts timed out in automatic approval review without a policy decision. CUA getApp also timed out, and no running fixture appeared in inventory. A sandboxed launch then returned:
+
+```text
+The application cannot be opened for an unexpected reason, error=Error Domain=NSOSStatusErrorDomain Code=-10827 "kLSNoExecutableErr: The executable is missing" UserInfo={_LSLine=4288, _LSFunction=_LSOpenStuffCallLocal, _LSFile=LSOpenCore.mm, _LSErrorMessage=kLSNoExecutableErr}
+```
+
+The copied Mach-O executable exists and is executable; this error is not evidence that its file is absent. Live enter/exit, menu hide and post-transition responsiveness are UNVERIFIED on the rebuilt fixture. UI3 is an implementation candidate, not accepted as Done. No real coding app or chat was read/written, no new permission or system setting was changed. Ibrahim can open the fixture from Finder to continue this check.
